DESCRIPTION:New technologies and organizations’ practices are regularly 
 placing pressures and challenges on our privacy and transparency rights. T
 his month\, Civic Tech Toronto and the Information and Privacy Commissione
 r of Ontario are highlighting the work to defend\, enforce\, explain and u
 pdate these rights. To see all September events\, check our events listing
  on Guild.&#x20\;\n\n‎‎\n\nTopic: Unnecessary and Disproportionate: Bi
 ll C-22 and Lawful Access\n\nThis talk will present on a recent joint brie
 f published by the Citizen Lab and the Canadian Civil Liberties Associatio
 n\, which analyzed the privacy and human rights implications of Bill C-22:
  An Act respecting lawful access.\n\nBrief: "(Un)forced Errors: An Analysi
 s of Proposed Surveillance Expansion under Bill C-22\, An Act respecting l
 awful access" by Cynthia Khoo\, Tamir Israel\, and Kate Robertson.\n\n‎
 ‎\n\nSpeaker: Cynthia Khoo \\[in-person]\n\nCynthia Khoo is a technology
  and human rights lawyer based in Toronto\, and a Senior Fellow at the Cit
 izen Lab. She has developed and provides\, through her legal practice and 
 research\, expertise specialized in how digital technologies impact the eq
 uality\, privacy\, and free expression rights of historically marginalized
  groups. / Website: tekhnoslaw.ca / Bluesky: cyn-k.bsky.social\n\n‎\n\nA
 genda:\n\n7:00-7:20 Welcome and Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Presentation an
 d Q\\&A/discussion\n\n7:50-9:00 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\nCode of Conduct:
